Objective
To understand how Windows Firewall rules control network traffic and investigate a firewall rule by examining its direction, action, protocol, port, scope, program, and service.
Practical Lab
I opened Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security using wf.msc and investigated the Remote Assistance (DCOM-In) firewall rule.
Rule Findings
Enabled: Yes
Action: Allow
Direction: Inbound
Profile: Domain
Protocol: TCP
Local Port: 135
Remote IP: Any
Program: svchost.exe
Service: RpcSs
Edge Traversal: Block
Investigation
The rule allows specific inbound TCP traffic through port 135. Port 135 is associated with Microsoft RPC (Remote Procedure Call) and DCOM functionality.
The traffic is associated with svchost.exe, which is a legitimate Windows host process, and the RpcSs service, which is the Remote Procedure Call service.
I also learned that a firewall rule allowing traffic does not automatically mean the traffic is malicious. The rule must be investigated based on its program, service, port, IP scope, profile, and purpose.
SOC-Style Practice
I investigated simulated firewall rules and identified a rule that required further investigation because it had:
An unknown executable
Inbound traffic allowed
TCP port 4444
A Public network profile
Any remote IP address
I learned that these indicators do not automatically prove that a rule is malicious. They indicate that further investigation is required.
